WebXR's `local` reference frame drifts relative to the real world when the user moves quickly or rotates the camera, causing placed AR objects (clue gems, trail pillars, treasure chest) to shift from their intended locations. All AR game objects now carry a GPS anchor and are re-projected on every GPS fix.

## Changes

- **`localToGPS(originLat, originLng, mx, mz)`** — inverse of the existing `gpsToLocal()`, linear approximation with sub-metre error up to ~500 m; uses named constant `METERS_PER_DEG_LAT = 111111`

- **`resyncARObjectsToGPS()`** — called at the end of every `onGPSUpdate()`; iterates `GAME.arClues`, `GAME.footprints`, and `GAME.treasureEntry` and recomputes each object's Three.js X/Z from its stored GPS anchor via `gpsToLocal()`

- **`spawnARClue()`** — when real GPS is available, derives initial Three.js position from the GPS fix instead of the XR reticle; computes and stores `gpsLat`/`gpsLng` via `localToGPS()` in all three placement paths (GPS, reticle, camera fallback)

- **`placeFootprint()`** — stores `GAME.lastGPS` as `gpsLat`/`gpsLng` on each pillar; falls back to `localToGPS()` in sim mode

- **`spawnARTreasureChest()`** — primary path now positions the chest directly from `GAME.treasureGPS` via `gpsToLocal()`, ensuring the chest spawns at the correct real-world coordinate rather than wherever the reticle lands

```js
// On every GPS fix, all live AR objects are snapped back to their GPS anchors:
function resyncARObjectsToGPS() {
  const origin = GAME.gpsPath[0];
  for (const clue of GAME.arClues) {
    if (clue.gpsLat == null) continue;
    const { mx, mz } = gpsToLocal(origin.lat, origin.lng, clue.gpsLat, clue.gpsLng);
    clue.mesh.position.x = mx;
    clue.mesh.position.z = mz;
    // light synced in tickGame() via light.position.copy(mesh.position)
  }
  // ... footprints and treasure chest follow same pattern
}
```

Sim/no-GPS mode is unaffected — `resyncARObjectsToGPS()` is only reachable via `onGPSUpdate()`, and all spawn fallback paths preserve existing behaviour.

